[THOUGHT] Amyloid PET was also not fulfilled. With 12/16 turns used and three high-yield biomarker tests (CSF, FDG-PET, amyloid PET) unavailable, I must pivot to the *next most discriminating clinical domain still incompletely assessed*: **Targeted physical examination**. While I previously requested it (Turn 7), no data was returned — but the supervisor’s feedback implies that objective signs could help differentiate bvFTD from mimics (e.g., absence of parkinsonism argues against corticobasal syndrome or PSP; presence of frontal release signs supports frontal lobe degeneration). Since I cannot assume exam was done, I must re-request the *most specific, observable, and discriminative elements* — focusing on signs with high specificity for bvFTD: bilateral grasp reflex, utilization behavior (e.g., handling objects spontaneously), and emotional blunting — all of which are readily assessable at bedside and do not require imaging or labs.

[TEST] Bedside neurological examination:  
1) Test for bilateral palmar grasp reflex (stroke fingers firmly across palms);  
2) Observe for utilization behavior (e.g., spontaneous handling of nearby objects like pen or stethoscope);  
3) Assess affect (e.g., congruence of facial expression with content, spontaneity of smiling/laughing during interview).

